[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Six months ago, we covered Pentest AGI at 11,700 stars. Today, it\'s past 23,000 and trending on GitHub all over again. It\'s a fully autonomous AI penetration testing agent. It plans its own steps,'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'runs 20 plus security tools in a sandbox, and writes the report. It\'s also grown up a lot since we last looked. So, here\'s the revisit and the one rule that matters more than any'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feature, authorized targets only. Before a single feature, the rule, because this is a dual-use tool and we treat it that way. Pentest AGI is for systems you own or are explicitly authorized in writing'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to test. Your own lab, a sanctioned engagement, a capture-the-flag range. Pointing an autonomous attacker at anything else is a crime, full stop, and no star count changes that. The good'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'news is the project\'s architecture actually pushes you toward doing this responsibly. It\'s self-hosted, so your data and scope stay yours, and every action runs inside an isolated Docker'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sandbox. With that established, what has Pentest AGI become? The numbers tell the trajectory. When we first covered it in March, Pentest AGI sat at 11,700 stars. It\'s now 23,821'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with over 3,000 forks. It has more than doubled its audience in 6 months, which for a niche security tool is a genuine signal, not a fluke spike. It\'s written'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in Go, MIT licensed, and the name is a statement of intent. Penetration Testing Artificial General Intelligence. The ambition hasn\'t shrunk. The tooling around it has just gotten a lot more'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'serious. The core loop is what makes it autonomous rather than a wrapper. You give it a scope you\'re authorized to test. The agent plans the steps, then executes them across a built-in suite of over 20 professional tools. Nmap for'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'discovery, SQL map for injection, Metasploit for exploitation, and more. With smart container management that pulls the right Docker image for each task. Optional execution monitoring and'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'task planning keep it on the rails. And crucially, everything happens in an isolated sandbox that has Docker without access to your host. That containment is not a nice to have on a tool like this.'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s the whole reason it\'s safe to run at all. On the site, you can see how the project frames itself now. Less lone hacker tool, more platform. There\'s a clean web UI, detailed reporting with'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exploitation guides, and a whole observability story with Grafana and Prometheus. The pitch has matured from an AI that hacks to a self-hosted autonomous testing platform your team'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can actually operate and audit. Which, for the professionals it targets, is the difference between a demo and something you\'d run in a real engagement. The architecture that grew the most is delegation. PentAGI isn\'t doing'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything. It\'s a team of specialists. A research agent gathers intelligence through a built-in browser and eight external search systems. Development and infrastructure agents handle their own'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'domains. A smart memory system stores successful approaches for next time. And an optional knowledge graph on Neo4j tracks the semantic relationships between findings. Every command and output persists in'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Postgres with vector search. It\'s less a script and more a small automated red team that remembers its past engagements. Here\'s the update that matters most for responsible use. The model layer exploded. PentAGI now'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'supports over 10 LLM providers. OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Bedrock, DeepSeek, Qwen, Kimmy, and more. Plus aggregators like Open Router. But the headline is'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local. There\'s a full guide for running it on VL LM with a quantized Quinn model entirely offline. Think about what that means for a security tool. Your target data, your findings, your exploitation notes, never'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leave your infrastructure. And there\'s no third-party logging what you tested. For authorized work under NDA, fully local inference isn\'t a nice option. It\'s often a hard requirement.'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Deployment is Docker Compose, which fits the self-hosted sandboxed philosophy. Configure your environment, your LLM provider keys or local endpoint, your search API keys, and bring the stack up.'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The web UI, the agent workers, and the Postgres vector store all come online together. From there, you log into the interface and define a task against a scope you control. I\'m not going to run'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a live test against anything in this video, because the only responsible target is one you\'re authorized on. And that\'s the entire point of it being self-hosted, so you\'re testing your lab, not the internet.'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, why the resurgence? Three things converged. The autonomous agent wave made an AI that does the whole job finally plausible instead of hype. The local model support arrived right as teams got serious about keeping'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sensitive testing off third-party APIs. And the platform matured. Reporting, monitoring, and APIs turned a research curiosity into something operable. Put those together and Pentest AGI stopped'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'being a novelty repo and started being a tool professionals bookmark. 24,000 stars is the market noticing. Let me be very direct about the two sides, because a tool this capable earns it. On the'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise side, this is a legitimately impressive piece of security engineering. Genuinely useful for authorized testing, CTF practice, and research. And the sandboxed self-hosted'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'design shows the maintainers thought about safety. On the responsibility side, a fully autonomous attacker lowers the skill floor for misuse, and pointing it at systems you don\'t own is illegal and'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unethical, no matter how easy the tool makes it. Use it on your own lab, on ranges built for it, or on engagements with signed authorization. The technology is neutral. What you aim it'

